The desk merged two date defect paths into one ticket. Shared subjects did not establish a shared cause. The old “known, one fix queued” conclusion cannot cover this evidence. Ticket coverage below follows the desk's exported filing view; symptom notes remain within their original group.

The report counts use the export through **4 October 2026**. The later engineering announcement is recorded separately below. Its reported completion is distinct from independent runtime verification, which was not performed here.

<DeskUpdate />

## Problems and report counts

<ReportAudit />

## Original defects and acceptance criteria

**Shared parser.** The supplied `causes.ts` documents month-first parsing of day-first tenant input. R-41203 describes 2 December becoming 12 February. R-41205 belongs here despite its `hint` intake tag: its tenant runs booking-v2 and its channel is app. The original fix criteria required the parsing contract to preserve day-first dates across affected entry paths. The desk now reports that correction fixed; this review did not independently reproduce the production behavior.

**Form hint.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form interprets input by its day-first tenant locale. The hint instructs the user to enter the wrong order. R-41230 belongs here despite a `parser` intake tag. The original requested change was to align visible guidance with interpretation. The desk instead retired booking-v3 and closed this problem without a fix; the earlier acceptance criteria are historical context, not active repair work.

## Unconfirmed symptom notes

The one-day reports quoted above describe calendar-day symptoms, including the same meeting appearing on different weekdays across cities. They are not a third cause in the exported model. Preserve invite payloads, calendar timezone, event timezone, and expected local date before proposing another defect. R-41236 does not itself establish a one-day move or a separate scheduling failure. The earlier speculative third ticket is closed; its evidence remains in these notes.

Some body evidence remains weak. R-41290, R-41417, R-41514, and R-41615 remain in the parser group by tenant/channel evidence, while their prose does not itself demonstrate an explicit transposed date pair. Keep that uncertainty visible rather than silently discarding reports or claiming every body proves a cause.

